n, which matches the visual findings. Although the model prediction [3] is high for IP (79.67%), the visual description [1] and the handbook [5] support this diagnosis. There are no precedent cases [4] to contradict this, and the diagnosis is not contradicted by the guidelines [2]. The other diagnoses (striae, EDS, scabies, prurigo nodularis) are less likely: striae are typically linear and associated with weight change; EDS presents with hyperextensibility and joint hypermobility; scabies causes vesicopustules and intense itching; prurigo nodularis presents with chronic nodules and scars. None of these match the diffuse, erythematous, hyperpigmented, and vascular findings seen in the image. Therefore, incontinentia pigmenti is the most likely diagnosis based on the visual findings, handbook knowledge, and absence of contradictory evidence.'}
