nal vinblastine, topical alitretinoin (9-cis-retinoic acid) gel, topical imiquimod, radiotherapy, laser surgery, or cryosurgery. Systemic options for widespread disease include IFN- $upalpha$ and multi-agent chemotherapy, e.g. anthracyclines (doxorubicin) or paclitaxel. The role of angiogenesis inhibitors and other drugs needs further investigation.  If immunodeficiency is corrected, e.g. by withdrawing or reducing immunosuppression, KS may regress. KS lesions also shrink in HiV-positive patients treated with highly active antiretroviral therapy (HAART). Localized disease may be controlled with intralesional vinblastine, topical alitretinoin (9-cis-retinoic acid) gel, topical imiquimod, radiotherapy, laser surgery, or cryosurgery. Systemic options for widespread disease include IFN- $\upalpha$ and multi-agent chemotherapy, e.g. anthracyclines (doxorubicin) or paclitaxel. The role of angiogenesis inhibitors and other drugs needs further investigation.

