[Handbook: drug induced pigmentary changes]
•    Mechanisms of hyperpigmentation include increased melanin synthesis and deposition of drug or drug metabolites in the skin. •   Photosensitivity may contribute to the colour change. •     Ask if the colour change was preceded by erythema or itching to differentiate drug-induced pigmentation from post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation, e.g. after dermatitis, lichenoid rashes, or fixed drug eruptions (see $spadesuit$ p. 378), particularly in individuals with dark skin. •     What is the colour and distribution of the change, e.g. is it predominantly in scars or on light-exposed skin?  •    Mechanisms of hyperpigmentation include increased melanin synthesis and deposition of drug or drug metabolites in the skin. •   Photosensitivity may contribute to the colour change. •     Ask if the colour change was preceded by erythema or itching to differentiate drug-induced pigmentation from post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation, e.g. after dermatitis, lichenoid rashes, or fixed drug eruptions (see $\spadesuit$ p. 378), particularly in individuals with dark skin. •     What is the colour and distribution of the change, e.g. is it predominantly in scars or on light-exposed skin?  •    Increased melanin (brown pigmentation) may be caused by drugs such as cytotoxics, hydroxycarbamide (hydroxyurea), pegylated IFN (in chronic hepatitis C infection), ACTH, oral contraceptives (melasma on the face), and some antiretroviral agents (zidovudine and lamivudine). •    Increased melanin (brown pigmentation) may be caused by drugs such as cytotoxics, hydroxycarbamide (hydroxyurea), pegylated IFN (in chronic hepatitis C infection), ACTH, oral contraceptives (melasma on the face), and some antiretroviral agents (zidovudine and lamivudine).
