nd, but the visual and guideline-based evidence is sufficient to confidently assign this diagnosis. The other diagnoses (neutrophilic dermatoses, neurodermatitis, lichen planus, sarcoidosis) are less likely: neutrophilic dermatoses typically present with acute inflammation and neutrophilic infiltration; neurodermatitis involves lichenoid or hyperpigmented plaques following Blaschko lines; lichen planus presents with flat-topped violaceous papules; and sarcoidosis typically presents with yellowish-brown papules or nodules, often with a psoriasiform appearance and diascopy revealing yellow-brown coloration — none of which are seen here. Therefore, necrobiosis lipoidica is the most likely diagnosis based on visual, guideline, and empirical reasoning."}
