ess. No precedent cases were found, but the visual and guideline-based evidence is sufficient to support this diagnosis over the other options. Erythema multiforme typically presents with symmetrical, target-like lesions, which is not observed here. Neutrophilic dermatoses are characterized by neutrophilic infiltration, often with painful ulcers or pustules, which are not the primary features here. Stevens-Johnson syndrome involves widespread epidermal necrosis and blistering, which is absent. Eczema typically presents with scaling, erythema, and vesicles, but lacks the vascular nodules and chronic atrophic changes seen in this case. Therefore, kaposi sarcoma is the most likely diagnosis based on the visual and guideline-based evidence."}
