ered by infection or drugs; photodermatoses are usually photosensitive and may show more chronic or atrophic changes. The model’s top prediction (acquired autoimmune bullous disease) is inconsistent with the visual findings, which lack blistering or bullae. The lack of precedent cases does not negate the diagnosis, as lichen planus is a common condition with variable presentations. The visual description and general handbook knowledge for lichen planus best match the current case, making it the most likely diagnosis.'}
