n arises de novo. The absence of a nodule (which would indicate deeper invasion) does not preclude the diagnosis of SSM, as it may be in the early or superficial phase. No precedent cases [4] were found, but the visual and guideline-based evidence is sufficient to support this diagnosis. The other diagnoses (BCC, nevus, morpheiform BCC, acrodermatitis) are less likely: BCC typically presents with a pearly border or telangiectasia in a more nodular or infiltrative pattern; nevus is usually symmetric and uniform; morpheiform BCC is waxy and indurated; acrodermatitis enteropathica presents with acral dermatitis and systemic symptoms. Therefore, the visual and guideline evidence strongly supports SSM as the primary diagnosis.'}
