nical features of Hailey-Hailey disease. Although the model [3] predicts Hailey-Hailey disease with 92.01% probability, the visual description [1] and the standard handbook [5] confirm that the key diagnostic features — acantholysis and dyskeratosis on biopsy — are the gold standard for differentiation, and the clinical presentation strongly supports this diagnosis. No precedent cases [4] were found, but the visual and guideline-based evidence is sufficient to confirm this diagnosis. The other diagnoses (lupus erythematosus, acne vulgaris, tuberous sclerosis, papilomatosis confluentes and reticulate) are excluded: lupus erythematosus typically presents with photosensitivity, discoid lesions, or systemic features; acne vulgaris involves comedones and pustules; tuberous sclerosis presents with hypopigmented macules and CMs; and papilomatosis confluentes and reticulate involves lichenoid or poikilodermatous lesions with specific nail or mucosal involvement — none of which are present here.'}
