n of prurigo nodularis, which often evolves from papules to chronic nodules and plaques, may show scarring, and can be asymmetric or segmental. The other top candidates from the model (Ehlers-Danlos Syndrome, Incontinentia Pigmenti, etc.) lack direct correlation with the described morphology and distribution. Ehlers-Danlos Syndrome is primarily defined by joint hypermobility and skin fragility, not chronic nodular plaques. Incontinentia Pigmenti typically presents with a specific sequence of lesions (vesicular, verrucous, hyperpigmented, hypopigmented) and is associated with X-linked inheritance and systemic features not seen here. Calcinosis cutis presents as hard nodules, often associated with systemic sclerosis or hyperparathyroidism, and lacks the described scaling and ulceration. Acrodermatitis enteropathica presents with acral vesiculobullous lesions and zinc deficiency, which is not suggested by the visual data. Therefore, despite the low AI probability, the visual and clinical features strongly support prurigo nodularis as the most likely diagnosis, and the absence of precedent cases does not negate this conclusion, as prurigo nodularis is a common chronic dermatosis with variable presentation.'}
