Hidradenitis suppurativa (acne inversa) is a chronic debilitating inflammation of the flexures, often misdiagnosed as staphylococcal furunculosis. Pathogenesis is uncertain, but follicular plugging may cause dilatation and rupture of follicles with $2^{circ}$ inflammation of apocrine glands. Hidradenitis is associated with obesity, polycystic ovary syndrome, and insulin resistance. Also associated with IBD, particularly Crohn disease. Cutaneous Crohn disease may simulate hidradenitis (see $pmb{triangleright}$ p. 508). Rarely associated with PG and acne (PASH syndrome) (see $spadesuit$ p. 234).  Hidradenitis suppurativa (acne inversa) is a chronic debilitating inflammation of the flexures, often misdiagnosed as staphylococcal furunculosis. Pathogenesis is uncertain, but follicular plugging may cause dilatation and rupture of follicles with $2^{\circ}$ inflammation of apocrine glands. Hidradenitis is associated with obesity, polycystic ovary syndrome, and insulin resistance. Also associated with IBD, particularly Crohn disease. Cutaneous Crohn disease may simulate hidradenitis (see $\pmb{\triangleright}$ p. 508). Rarely associated with PG and acne (PASH syndrome) (see $\spadesuit$ p. 234).
