[Handbook: perioral dermatitis]
Perioral dermatitis is an inflammatory condition that occurs in children and adults, usually young and middle-aged women. The cause is unknown, but exogenous factors, such as potent topical corticosteroids (see $spadesuit$ p. 657), inhaled corticosteroids, irritant cosmetics, fluorinated toothpastes, and occlusive emollients, have been implicated.  Perioral dermatitis is an inflammatory condition that occurs in children and adults, usually young and middle-aged women. The cause is unknown, but exogenous factors, such as potent topical corticosteroids (see $\spadesuit$ p. 657), inhaled corticosteroids, irritant cosmetics, fluorinated toothpastes, and occlusive emollients, have been implicated.  The name is misleading. Perioral skin is affected more often than other sites, but the condition may affect periocular and perinasal skin. It has also been reported on the trunk. Nor is perioral dermatitis a typical dermatitis; it is more akin to rosacea. Dermatitis is treated with a topical corticosteroid; yet topical corticosteroids are one of the causes of perioral dermatitis.  The name is misleading. Perioral skin is affected more often than other sites, but the condition may affect periocular and perinasal skin. It has also been reported on the trunk. Nor is perioral dermatitis a typical dermatitis; it is more akin to rosacea. Dermatitis is treated with a topical corticosteroid; yet topical corticosteroids are one of the causes of perioral dermatitis.
