nostic feature is the presence of hyperpigmented, scaly plaques in flexural areas — which is precisely what is observed. The lesion's chronic, inflammatory appearance and bilateral symmetry are consistent with the typical presentation of acanthosis nigricans, especially in the context of insulin resistance, which is often associated with this condition. Although the AI model [3] predicts syringoma with 90.82% probability, syringomas are typically discrete, flesh-colored or yellowish papules, often found on the face or neck, and do not present as confluent, scaly, erythematous plaques in the perineum. The visual description [1] and the handbook entry for acanthosis nigricans align strongly, and there are no precedent cases [4] to contradict this. Therefore, despite the AI's high confidence in syringoma, the visual and clinical evidence strongly supports acanthosis nigricans as the most likely diagnosis."}
