nce from the handbook is sufficient to support this diagnosis over the other options, which lack the specific features of chronic flexural inflammation, abscess formation, and associated signs of insulin resistance (e.g., acanthosis nigricans, obesity) that are often seen in hidradenitis. The other diagnoses (lupus subacute, psoriasis, keloid, mucous cyst) do not fit the clinical presentation as well: lupus subacute typically presents with non-scarring lesions or discoid rashes, psoriasis usually shows well-demarcated plaques with scaling, keloids are smooth nodular scars, and mucous cysts are typically solitary, punctate lesions. Therefore, despite the low model probability, the visual and guideline-based evidence strongly supports hidradenitis as the primary diagnosis."}
