[Handbook: lupus erythematosus]
- Acute cutaneous lupus: lupus malar rash (do not count if malar discoid), bullous lupus, TEN variant of SLE, maculopapular lupus rash, photosensitive lupus rash, or subacute cutaneous lupus (nonindurated psoriaform and/or annular polycyclic lesions that resolve without scarring, although occasionally with post-inflammatory dyspigmentation or telangiectasias).   
- Chronic cutaneous LE: classical discoid rash (localized——above the neck; generalized—-above and below the neck), hypertrophic (verrucous) lupus, lupus panniculitis (profundus), mucosal lupus, LE tumidus, chilblain lupus, discoid lupus/LP overlap.   
- Oral ulcers: palate (buccal, tongue) or nasal ulcers (in the absence of other causes).   
- Non-scarring alopecia.   
- Synovitis: ${ge}2$ joints, characterized by swelling or effusion or tenderness in ${ge}2$ joints and ${geq}30mathrm{min}$ of morning stiffness.   
- Serositis: typical pleurisy for ${>}1$ day, or pleural effusions, or pleural rub or typical pericardial pain for ${>}1$ day, or pericardial effusion, or pericardial rub, or pericarditis by ECG.   
- Renal: urine protein:creatinine ratio (or 24-h urine protein) representing $500mathrm{mg}$ of protein/24h or RBC casts.   
- Neurological: seizures, psychosis, mononeuritis multiplex, myelitis, peripheral or cranial neuropathy, acute confusional state (in the absence of other causes).   
- Haemolytic anaemia.   
- Leucopenia $(<4000/mathrm{mm}^{3}$ at least once) or lymphopenia $(<1000/$ $mathsf{m}mathsf{m}^{3}$ at least once).   
- Thrombocytopenia $(<100000/mathrm{mm}^{3})$ at least once.  
Immunological criteria  Immunological criteria  - ANA above reference range.   
- Anti-dsDNA above reference range or $2{times}2$ above if enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA).   
