Vitiligo is a common acquired skin condition in which epidermal melanocytes are lost, leading to complete loss of pigment, i.e. depigmentation, rather than hypopigmentation (partial pigment loss) (see Fig. 22.5). Average age of onset is 20 years, but vitiligo may commence in childhood. Up to a third report a family history of vitiligo. Psychological impact may be profound, particularly in individuals with darkly pigmented skin. Vitiligo is associated with various autoimmune diseases, including thyroid disease (see Box 22.19).  Vitiligo is a common acquired skin condition in which epidermal melanocytes are lost, leading to complete loss of pigment, i.e. depigmentation, rather than hypopigmentation (partial pigment loss) (see Fig. 22.5). Average age of onset is 20 years, but vitiligo may commence in childhood. Up to a third report a family history of vitiligo. Psychological impact may be profound, particularly in individuals with darkly pigmented skin. Vitiligo is associated with various autoimmune diseases, including thyroid disease (see Box 22.19).

