tion aligns strongly with its clinical presentation. The handbook [5] confirms that granuloma annulare is a chronic non-infectious inflammatory condition that can present with extensive, symmetric lesions and is sometimes associated with necrobiosis lipoidica, which may explain the hyperpigmentation and thickened skin. Although no precedent cases [4] are available, the visual and clinical features match the known presentation of granuloma annulare more closely than any other listed diagnosis. Allergic contact dermatitis typically presents with more acute, erythematous, and vesicular features, and is often associated with a clear history of exposure. Pityriasis rosea is self-limited and typically presents with a herald patch followed by a more generalized rash, not chronic hyperkeratotic plaques. Photodermatoses usually present with sun-induced lesions, and epidermal nevi are typically linear or follow Blaschko lines, not symmetrically distributed on the plantar surfaces. Therefore, granuloma annulare is the most likely diagnosis based on the visual findings and clinical standards."}
