ort this diagnosis over the other options. Lupus erythematosus and dermatomyositis typically present with more acute, photosensitive, or systemic features (e.g., malar rash, joint pain, or muscle weakness) not evident here. Nematode infections usually present with more localized, pruritic, or nodular lesions, not chronic erosive plaques. Lupus subacute is also unlikely as it typically presents with non-scarring, psoriasiform or annular lesions that resolve without scarring. The chronic, erosive, and hyperpigmented nature of the lesions in this case is most consistent with epidermolysis bullosa, even without precedent cases [4] to confirm, as the visual and clinical features match the known presentation of EB.'}
