del [3] assigns a high probability (57.59%) to 'solid cystic basal cell carcinoma', the visual description and handbook criteria strongly align with this diagnosis. The model's prediction is therefore validated by the clinical and visual evidence. No precedent cases [4] were found, but the empirical evidence from the handbook is sufficient to confidently assign this diagnosis. The lesion does not match the criteria for squamous cell carcinoma (lack of scaly or ulcerated features), nevocytic nevus (lack of pigment network or globules), or dermatofibroma (lack of brownish-red dermal nodule appearance)."}
