nosis (8.09%) compared to basal cell carcinoma (28.05%), the visual description and handbook criteria for BCC morpheiform are more specific and comprehensive in matching the lesion's morphology. The other diagnoses are less likely: seborrheic keratosis is typically well-defined, non-ulcerated, and does not present with necrotic debris or irregular borders; neutrophilic dermatoses are usually associated with acute inflammation and systemic symptoms not suggested here; nevus sebaceous of Jadassohn is typically present at birth or early childhood and follows Blaschko lines, which is not indicated. The lesion's features are classic for BCC morpheiform, and no precedent cases were found to contradict this diagnosis."}
