ts with firm, yellowish-brown papules or plaques on an erythematous background, which does not match; psoriasis usually presents with well-demarcated, erythematous plaques with silvery scale on extensor surfaces, often symmetric; halo nevus presents with a linear or checkerboard pattern of pigmentation, not scaly plaques; and porokeratosis of Mibelli presents with a distinctive 'cornoid lamella' and often a bright white tone under Wood's light, which is not described here. Therefore, the visual findings and clinical guidelines support a diagnosis of lupus erythematosus."}
